Subject: [PATCH 63/63] kernel: dict-limit and dict-used -- the two cells here! compares, readable from Forth, so a dictionary budget can be asked for before it is spent

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

PREBOOT_STRUCTURE_TOOLKIT_LAB_PLAN.md section 6 recorded, honestly, that the
dictionary budget was never measured and that measuring it would be the first
task of any spike wanting the dsl compiled into the firmware. Measuring it needs
two numbers the kernel has and Forth could not read: dictlimit (what the arch's
init sized the dictionary to) and dicthead (how much of it is spent). `here` is
dict + dicthead, but dict itself is not published, and the limit was reachable
only by ALLOTTING PAST IT and reading the line here! prints:

    Dictionary space overflow: dicthead=... dictlimit=...

That line is also the whole of the kernel's protection. herewrite() prints it and
CONTINUES; the dictionary is a static array (arch/x86: 1 MiB; amd64 and unix:
256 KiB; ppc/qemu: 384 KiB) and the next `,` past its end lands in whatever .bss
follows. A budget therefore has to be asked for BEFORE compiling, which is what
this patch makes possible and what smoke-openbios.sh's dict-budget track does:
a file is evaluated only if 3 x its source size fits in dict-limit dict-used -.

Two kernel primitives, appended to words[] (kernel/forth.c) and wordnames[]
(kernel/bootstrap.c) in the same order:

    dict-limit ( -- n )   dictlimit
    dict-used  ( -- n )   dicthead

Kernel, so every arch has them from the bootstrap on; no arch file changes.

MEASURED (the track's controls, every arch): `room a + dup allot negate allot`
prints the overflow line and its dictlimit= equals what dict-limit answered;
`room a - dup allot negate allot` prints nothing; dict-used at boot equals the
.dict file's header length field read on the host -- the number kernel/dict.c
copies into dicthead when it loads the dictionary.

diff --git a/kernel/forth.c b/kernel/forth.c
--- a/kernel/forth.c
+++ b/kernel/forth.c
@@ -1669,6 +1669,30 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ *  dict-limit  ( -- n )   mklab (patch 63): the dictionary's size in bytes --
+ *  dict-used   ( -- n )   and how many of them are spent. THE RUNNING BINARY'S
+ *  NUMBERS, not the source constant: dictlimit is whatever the arch's init set
+ *  (arch/x86: 1 MiB; amd64, unix: 256 KiB; ppc/qemu: 384 KiB; sparc: from
+ *  sys_info), and dicthead is what here! compares against it. Without these the
+ *  only way to learn the limit from Forth was to allot past it and read the
+ *  "Dictionary space overflow" line -- which is also the one check here! makes,
+ *  and it PRINTS and continues: the dictionary is a static array and the next
+ *  `,` past its end lands in whatever .bss follows. So a toolkit that wants to
+ *  live in the dictionary needs a budget it can ask for BEFORE it compiles, and
+ *  smoke-openbios.sh's dict-budget track measures that budget on every arch.
+ */
+
+static void dictlimit_word(void)
+{
+	PUSH((ucell)dictlimit);
+}
+
+static void dictused_word(void)
+{
+	PUSH((ucell)dicthead);
+}
+
+/*
  *  here!       ( new-dict-pointer -- )
  */
 
@@ -1978,4 +2002,6 @@
     do_encode_file,         /* $encode-file */
     do_debug_xt,            /* (debug  */
     do_debug_off,           /* (debug-off) */
+    dictlimit_word,         /* dict-limit  (mklab, patch 63) */
+    dictused_word,          /* dict-used   (mklab, patch 63) */
 };
diff --git a/kernel/bootstrap.c b/kernel/bootstrap.c
--- a/kernel/bootstrap.c
+++ b/kernel/bootstrap.c
@@ -89,7 +89,8 @@
 	"here", "here!", "dobranch", "do?branch", "unaligned-w@",
 	"unaligned-w!", "unaligned-l@", "unaligned-l!", "ioc@", "iow@",
 	"iol@", "ioc!", "iow!", "iol!", "i", "j", "call", "sys-debug",
-	"$include", "$encode-file", "(debug", "(debug-off)"
+	"$include", "$encode-file", "(debug", "(debug-off)",
+	"dict-limit", "dict-used"	/* mklab, patch 63 -- same order as forth.c's words[] */
 };
 
